\ core::cmp -- the typed comparisons over a nominal `Ord` trait.
\
\ `Ord` is an ordinary library trait: `cmp ( 'T 'T -- Ordering )`, satisfied
\ nominally by one `impl:` block per numeric width. The six surface
\ comparisons (`eq`/`lt`/`gt`/`lte`/`gte`/`ne`) are derived from `cmp` and its
\ three-variant result, so the compiler itself needs no reserved notion of
\ "ordered" at all -- a user type opts in with its own `impl: Ord`, exactly as
\ any other trait.
\
\ The comparisons are deliberately **not** `inline` here (P7.S3s R5): an
\ `inline` word may declare no `Bound::User` variable at all
\ (`reject_user_bound_on_combinator`), and `Ord` is now one. Each comparison
\ is therefore a real call with a frame, a measured ~2x tax over the old
\ branch-and-construct body on a comparison-heavy loop, accepted for this
\ slice (P7.S3o names the follow-on that could restore the splice).
import: intrinsics i | ueq ult ugt ulte ugte une branch drop | ;
import: self::bool b | Bool False True | ;

export: Ord Ordering Less Equal Greater eq lt gt lte gte ne ;

type: Ordering
| Less
| Equal
| Greater
;

trait: Ord 'T
  cmp ( 'T 'T -- Ordering )
;

\ The eight fixed-width integers (`INT_TYPES`, `src/ast.rs`) and
\ `usize`/`isize`: `ult`/`ugt` derive signed/unsigned/float behaviour from
\ the operand type at lowering, so one two-comparison body covers every
\ width -- `a < b` is `Less`, `a > b` is `Greater`, otherwise `Equal`. `branch`
\ reads the raw 32-bit flag `ult`/`ugt` themselves produce, the same
\ primitive `core::bool`'s `if` is built over, so no comparison here goes
\ through the `Bool` it has not yet built.
impl: Ord for i8
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i16
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i32
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i64
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u8
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u16
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u32
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u64
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for usize
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for isize
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

\ Both floats. A NaN operand is the one case `Ordering`'s three variants
\ cannot represent: IEEE-754 makes every ordered comparison false, including
\ `x = x`, so a partial order needs a fourth "unordered" answer this enum has
\ no arm for. `Equal` is the arm a NaN lands in here (neither `ult` nor `ugt`
\ holds), which makes `eq` answer `True` for two NaNs where the raw `ueq`
\ answers `False`. A total `Ord` over a partial order is the known cost of
\ satisfying the numeric tower nominally; a `PartialOrd`/`Ordering?`-shaped
\ split is the follow-on, not this slice.
impl: Ord for f32
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for f64
  : cmp
    | a b |
    a b ult [ Less ] [ a b ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

: eq  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop False ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: lt  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: gt  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop False ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop True ]
  Ordering? ;

: lte ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: gte ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop False ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop True ]
  Ordering? ;

: ne  ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop True ]
  Ordering? ;
